We divided our Collage Application into three sections; adhering to the Model, View, Controller pattern in
Software design.

We first decided to create interfaces for the model, view and controller. These interfaces hold all the
necessary requirements and functionalities that allow our program to work accordingly.

We initially had one interface in our model that housed all the methods necessary to run our program but
as we developed more understanding of the requirements, we felt that it would be best to
have two different interfaces that are associated through inheritance.

The CollageProjectModel interface holds the general methods that can be used for any implementation of a similar
application. The CollageProject interface holds the methods that are specific to this particular implementation
of our application.

Housed in our model, is the Effects package which contains the MacroCollageEffects. This interface is our macro
and we came to an agreement that since macros are a scalable way to create a growing extended set of
functionality, it would be best to use the command design pattern to
 support a set of effects/ filters that our program offers. This way, our design is loosely
coupled and if we have to add more operations(effects), we can just add new classes that perform them.

Our View interface is the CollageView and our controller interface is the CollageController.

These interfaces work together together to create our Collaging application and to deliver the
functionalities that were required.
They are organized such that they allow for extensibility in the cases where we are asked to implement
more functionality.

These interfaces are implemented by :
+ The CollageProjectModelImpl
+ The CollageControllerImpl
+ The CollageTextView


We have stand alone classes which include the

 + Pixel class : It represents a single pixel that makes up images. We have fields that represent each
 red, green , blue and alpha component of a Pixel. Our constructor ensures that invalid arguments are not
 accepted; this way we make sure that all Pixel values are correct. We have observers that return each of
 these components when we need them ; these were included for many reasons including testing purposes.
 We also have three main methods that can alter the appearance of a pixel and added methods on a need-basis.

 + Layer class : In this class, we have methods and fields that are necessary for the required layer implementations

 + ImageUtil class : The class provided to us

 + Main : Our main methods; which when run accepts command line arguments that allow the program to be used.

+ executes macro should take in a layer because it is easy to understand from a users point of view.



